{"step_index":974,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:29:11Z","thinking":"**Examining Candidate Theorem Implications**\n\nThe `two_le_romanDominationNumber` result is confirmed, representing a good step forward. The next question focuses on how this connects to the `Candidate` theorem. Revisiting `Candidate` requires careful consideration of its conditions and the implications when `radius G ≥ 2`. The reasoning aims to determine if the `Candidate` is generally true under this condition.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\nimport networkx as nx\n\n# Check if there is any graph where radius + 1 > roman_domination_number\n# Let us test paths P_n, cycles C_n, trees, etc.\n\ndef roman_domination_number(G):\n    # solve using integer programming or brute force for small graphs\n    import scipy.optimize\n    # f(v) in {0, 1, 2}\n    # min sum f(v)\n    # s.t. for all v with f(v) = 0, sum_{u in N(v)} [f(u) == 2] >= 1\n    # We can use binary variables x_v (f(v)>=1), y_v (f(v)==2).\n    # Then f(v) = x_v + y_v.\n    # If f(v) == 0, then x_v = 0, y_v = 0.\n    # Roman domination condition: x_v + sum_{u in N(v)} y_u >= 1.\n    # exclusion: y_v <= x_v.\n    # objective: sum (x_v + y_v).\n    import pulp\n    prob = pulp.LpProblem(\"RDN\", pulp.LpMinimize)\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    x = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"x_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n    y = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"y_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n    prob += pulp.lpSum(x[v] + y[v] for v in nodes)\n    for v in nodes:\n        prob += y[v] <= x[v]\n        prob += x[v] + pulp.lpSum(y[u] for u in G.neighbors(v)) >= 1\n    prob.solve(pulp.PULP_CBC_CMD(msg=False))\n    return int(round(pulp.value(prob.objective)))\n\nfor n in range(2, 30):\n    P = nx.path_graph(n)\n    rad = nx.radius(P)\n    rdn = roman_domination_number(P)\n    if rad + 1 > rdn:\n        print(f\"COUNTEREXAMPLE P_{n}: rad={rad}, rdn={rdn}\")\n    C = nx.cycle_graph(n)\n    rad_c = nx.radius(C)\n    rdn_c = roman_domination_number(C)\n    if rad_c + 1 > rdn_c:\n        print(f\"COUNTEREXAMPLE C_{n}: rad={rad_c}, rdn={rdn_c}\")\nprint(\"Paths and cycles up to 30 tested.\")\n'","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Testing paths and cycles in python","toolSummary":"Check paths and cycles"}}]}
